ASCII string microbenchmarks
============================

Measure the cost of Unicon string operations on **pure ASCII** (untagged)
strings, with Unicode compiled **off** vs **on**.

These are not pass/fail tests. Each program times one operation and prints
a single line:

    strascii.size  unicode=off  n=2000000  cpu_ms=42  wall_ms=43

``cpu_ms`` is ``&time`` (CPU milliseconds). ``wall_ms`` is elapsed
time from ``gettimeofday()``, the same pairing as ``tests/bench``.
``unicode=on`` means this interpreter was built with ``--enable-unicode``
(``Unicode`` is in ``&features``). The strings themselves are still ASCII,
so the tagged UTF-8 paths should not run -- this is the tax on the
historic string representation.

Programs
--------

- ``size``         ``*s``
- ``index``        ``s[i]`` (every character)
- ``concat``       ``a || b`` (fixed operands)
- ``concat-grow``  ``s ||:= piece`` (growing)
- ``slice``        ``s[i:j]``
- ``bang``         ``!s``
- ``scan``         ``move(1)`` over ``&subject``
- ``tab``          ``tab(&pos+1)`` over ``&subject``
- ``find``         ``find(needle, haystack)``
- ``match``        ``match(prefix, s)``
- ``reverse``      ``reverse(s)``

Optional first argument is the iteration count. Defaults are sized so
each program runs several seconds (the whole suite about a minute),
long enough that a few milliseconds of noise is a fraction of a
percent rather than tens of percent.

How to compare two builds
-------------------------

Build Unicon twice, into two prefixes (or sequentially, saving ``bin/``):

    ./configure && make
    # save bin/unicon and bin/iconx, or install to prefix A

    ./configure --enable-unicode && make
    # prefix B

From this directory, using each interpreter in turn:

    make clean
    make UC=/path/to/off/unicon
    make run UC=/path/to/off/unicon

    make clean
    make UC=/path/to/on/unicon
    make run UC=/path/to/on/unicon

Or without make:

    unicon -s size.icn && ./size
    ...

Compare the ``cpu_ms=`` and ``wall_ms=`` values for the same program and
the same ``n=``. Do not treat a single run as gospel; noise of a few
percent is normal. The interesting result is whether ASCII-only work is
slower when Unicode support is compiled in.

Compare the ``ms=`` columns in UTR 25 section 10.2 after both builds
have been timed with these defaults.
